@jjallaire @jcheng5 it would be good to get your thoughts on this.

We've had some discussions internally about how functions which return closures from within packages can cause breakage when package B is built against one version of package A (which returns it a closure) and is then run against a different version of package A. The issues seems to have to do with package namespaces being included within closures that are returned.

This change reverts to the original "delegating" model for shiny output/render functions. The one twist is that as a result of how quoting is handled there is one additional line of boilerplate required when defining a shiny render function for a widget. Here's what the new boilerplate looks like:

``` S
simpleNetworkOutput <- function(outputId, width = "100%", height = "500px") {
  shinyWidgetOutput(outputId, "simpleNetwork", width, height, package = "networkD3")
}

renderSimpleNetwork <- function(expr, env = parent.frame(), quoted = FALSE) {
  if (!quoted) { expr <- substitute(expr) } # force quoted
  shinyRenderWidget(expr, simpleNetworkOutput, env, quoted = TRUE)
}
```

Original source here: https://github.com/christophergandrud/networkD3/blob/feature/shiny-function-delegation/R/simpleNetwork.R#L100-L111

While I think this extra line of boilerplate is regrettable, I think that (a) shielding ourselves from hard to diagnose package versioning issues is well worth it; and (b) this is code that will be seen only by widget developers rather than end users.

@jcheng5  or @hadley, curious if you think there is a way to eliminate the boilerplate without reverting to the "factory function" approach (thus exposing us to the package versioning issues).

This is a proposal for unifying the little JavaScript snippet each widget author needs to include. Rather than having one codepath for static rendering and one codepath for Shiny, you can use this simplified API to support both at once:

``` r
HTMLWidgets.widget({
  name: "knob",
  initialize: function(el) {
    $(el).knob();
  },
  renderValue: function(el, data) {
    $(el).trigger("configure", data);
    $(el).val(data.value).trigger("change");
  }
});
```

This example doesn't show it, but if `initialize` returns a value, it's passed as a third argument to `renderValue`.

**Open questions/issue:**
- [x] If it's likely we'll have input bindings in the future, then perhaps `HTMLWidgets.widget` should be renamed `.outputWidget`, or something like `widgetType: "output"` or `output: true` should be required on the object.
- [x] The `widgetOutput` function uses `widgetname_output` rather than just `widgetname`. I didn't include it in this PR but to get the knob example working, I had to change it to just `widgetname`. Maybe you can help me understand the indented semantics of `widget_div`/`widgetOutput`.
- [x] Where should package authors be told to place their binding.js files? It can't be right in inst, it needs to be a subdirectory, otherwise we will copy the entire package into their dependencies directory at static render time.

